EASE-001 for a real artifact to install; until then this\nrow may run against the clone and its findings stand.", "status": "pending", "title": "The documented install path has never been exercised end-to-end on a clean box (AUG-048 records the dogfood install leg as SKIPPED) \u2014 verify install -> init -> start -> ask on a bare machine and file what breaks", "updated_at": "2026-09-23T23:26:02.644154+00:00"}
-{"id": "AUG-055", "title": "answer accepts a --chosen that matches no --option: a success line over a configuration that selects NOTHING", "priority": "P1", "complexity": 2, "status": "pending", "reasoning": "WHY (found live 2026-09-24, 5th dogfood run, namespace df5-real)\n\nThe single most damaging defect a user hits, because it is silent and it defeats\nthe loop's last step.\n\n`answer` inserts its option rows with `active = (opt == a.chosen)` \u2014 a VERBATIM\nSTRING EQUALITY between the --chosen text and the --option text (auger.py\ncmd_answer, the option insert). Nothing validates the pair, nothing warns, and\nthe pair is easy to miss because `--option` is naturally a full sentence while\n--chosen is naturally a short name for it.\n\nReproduced verbatim:\n  answer --question-id Q-000001 --chosen \"The CI box smoke test\" \\\n         --option \"CI smoke battery: bunker3 runs the project's own documented\n                   smoke check ...\" --why-not \"...\"\n  -> D-001 recorded  (confidence -1.0, 1 options, embedded, scope project)\n     closed Q-000001\n  dump\n  -> ACTIVE CONFIGURATION: (nothing active)\n     WARNING: decisions with != 1 active option:\n       - D-001: 0 of 1 options active \u2014 this decision contributes NOTHING\n\nEvery subsequent verb inherits the damage: `dump --config` then compares the\nhypothesis against a record whose chosen text is not any option, so it reports a\nspurious CONTRADICTION (\"choosing <option label> contradicts the recorded choice\n(<the different sentence>)\") for a hypothesis that actually agrees with it.\n\nThe documentation invites this: docs/VERBS.md says `--option` is \"repeatable \u2014\nevery alternative, chosen ones included\" and `--chosen` is \"the choice\", with no\nstatement that the two must be byte-identical.\n\nDELIVERABLE (pick one, but the first two are the fix)\n- validate that --chosen resolves to exactly one of the supplied --option values\n  before anything is stored, refusing by name otherwise (the resolve_option\n  machinery already exists and already refuses loudly); OR\n- make --chosen accept an option token (D-001-O3 / O3 / a label) and derive the\n  chosen label from the option row, so the two can never disagree; AND\n- whatever the mechanism, the success line must state the resulting active-option\n  count (\"D-001 recorded (1 of 3 options active)\"), so an empty configuration can\n  never be printed as a clean success.\n\nACCEPTANCE CRITERIA\n- `answer --chosen <text not among --option>` exits non-zero naming the mismatch,\n  OR is accepted with the chosen option auto-activated \u2014 and in either case stores\n  exactly one active option\n- the recorded decision renders in `dump` with exactly one active option and NO\n  spurious contradiction line in `dump --config` for a hypothesis that matches it\n- the success line reports the active-option count\n- a regression test covers the mismatched-wording case (chosen a sentence, option\n  a longer sentence, real use)\n- ruff check auger.py clean", "capability_tags": ["+engine", "+decision"], "attempts": 0, "blocks": [], "depends_on": [], "detail": {}, "files_changed": [], "foreman_note": null, "guard_result": null, "ci_result": null, "commit_hash": null, "perpetual": null, "created_at": "2026-09-24T03:55:24.297805+00:00", "updated_at": "2026-09-24T03:55:24.297805+00:00"}
+{"id": "AUG-055", "title": "answer accepts a --chosen that matches no --option: a success line over a configuration that selects NOTHING", "priority": "P1", "complexity": 2, "status": "in_progress", "reasoning": "WHY (found live 2026-09-24, 5th dogfood run, namespace df5-real)\n\nThe single most damaging defect a user hits, because it is silent and it defeats\nthe loop's last step.\n\n`answer` inserts its option rows with `active = (opt == a.chosen)` \u2014 a VERBATIM\nSTRING EQUALITY between the --chosen text and the --option text (auger.py\ncmd_answer, the option insert). Nothing validates the pair, nothing warns, and\nthe pair is easy to miss because `--option` is naturally a full sentence while\n--chosen is naturally a short name for it.\n\nReproduced verbatim:\n  answer --question-id Q-000001 --chosen \"The CI box smoke test\" \\\n         --option \"CI smoke battery: bunker3 runs the project's own documented\n                   smoke check ...\" --why-not \"...\"\n  -> D-001 recorded  (confidence -1.0, 1 options, embedded, scope project)\n     closed Q-000001\n  dump\n  -> ACTIVE CONFIGURATION: (nothing active)\n     WARNING: decisions with != 1 active option:\n       - D-001: 0 of 1 options active \u2014 this decision contributes NOTHING\n\nEvery subsequent verb inherits the damage: `dump --config` then compares the\nhypothesis against a record whose chosen text is not any option, so it reports a\nspurious CONTRADICTION (\"choosing <option label> contradicts the recorded choice\n(<the different sentence>)\") for a hypothesis that actually agrees with it.\n\nThe documentation invites this: docs/VERBS.md says `--option` is \"repeatable \u2014\nevery alternative, chosen ones included\" and `--chosen` is \"the choice\", with no\nstatement that the two must be byte-identical.\n\nDELIVERABLE (pick one, but the first two are the fix)\n- validate that --chosen resolves to exactly one of the supplied --option values\n  before anything is stored, refusing by name otherwise (the resolve_option\n  machinery already exists and already refuses loudly); OR\n- make --chosen accept an option token (D-001-O3 / O3 / a label) and derive the\n  chosen label from the option row, so the two can never disagree; AND\n- whatever the mechanism, the success line must state the resulting active-option\n  count (\"D-001 recorded (1 of 3 options active)\"), so an empty configuration can\n  never be printed as a clean success.\n\nACCEPTANCE CRITERIA\n- `answer --chosen <text not among --option>` exits non-zero naming the mismatch,\n  OR is accepted with the chosen option auto-activated \u2014 and in either case stores\n  exactly one active option\n- the recorded decision renders in `dump` with exactly one active option and NO\n  spurious contradiction line in `dump --config` for a hypothesis that matches it\n- the success line reports the active-option count\n- a regression test covers the mismatched-wording case (chosen a sentence, option\n  a longer sentence, real use)\n- ruff check auger.py clean", "capability_tags": ["+engine", "+decision"], "attempts": 0, "blocks": [], "depends_on": [], "detail": {}, "files_changed": [], "foreman_note": null, "guard_result": null, "ci_result": null, "commit_hash": null, "perpetual": null, "created_at": "2026-09-24T03:55:24.297805+00:00", "updated_at": "2026-09-24T06:21:34.822094+00:00", "worker_status": "running", "worker_summary": "Worker gpt-5.6-luna@openai-codex dispatched by auger-2026-09-24-06-16-22; process proc_7a2c345af901; lane verified by session_model_usage session 20260924_011928_19d4d3."}
 {"id": "AUG-056", "title": "ask re-proposes the question it just closed: the already-answered gate does not see a closed question and wastes a full round", "priority": "P1", "complexity": 2, "status": "pending", "reasoning": "WHY (found live 2026-09-24, 5th dogfood run, namespace df5-real)\n\n`ask` proposed the IDENTICAL question text in the round immediately after the\nuser answered exactly that question.\n\nObserved sequence:\n  ask                                  -> stored Q-000001  What test proves this works?\n  answer --question-id Q-000001 ...    -> D-001 recorded, closed Q-000001\n  ask                                  -> already answered? 0.3 -> genuinely new\n                                          stored Q-000002  What test proves this works?\n  answer --question-id Q-000002 ...    -> D-002 recorded  (a DUPLICATE decision)\n  ask                                  -> question not stored: JEV proposed none:\n                                          nothing left worth asking\n\nSo the drill burned one ask (5.0s + a model call), one answer, and one duplicate\ndecision to close a question the previous answer had already closed. The loop\nself-heals only AFTER the waste.\n\nRoot cause is in the gate, not in JEV: the already-answered check scores the\nproposed question text against embedded evidence, and the evidence for D-001 was\nwritten with a chosen sentence that differs from the option label \u2014 so the noul\nscore came back 0.24-0.30 against a threshold above it. Note this is COMPOUNDED\nby AUG-055 (a mismatched --chosen also degrades the evidence text the gate reads),\nbut is not caused by it: the gate has no signal for \"the question this text came\nfrom was closed one round ago\" even when the evidence is clean.\n\nDELIVERABLE\n- carry the closed-question identity: when `answer --question-id Q` closes Q, the\n  gate must exclude Q (and the proposal text it carried) from the next round's\n  askable set \u2014 for at least one round \u2014 rather than relying on a similarity score\n  over evidence prose\n- OR record the closed question id on the decision's evidence entry and have the\n  already-answered pass treat an exact closed-question-text match as answered\n  regardless of the noul score\n\nACCEPTANCE CRITERIA\n- the documented loop (ask -> answer -> ask) never re-proposes the question text\n  that the previous answer closed, on a fresh namespace, reproduced as a test\n- a duplicate decision is NOT created to close a re-proposed twin\n- the second ask still works normally when the closed question was a DIFFERENT\n  text (the gate must not simply suppress the next question)\n- ruff check auger.py clean", "capability_tags": ["+engine", "+method"], "attempts": 0, "blocks": [], "depends_on": [], "detail": {}, "files_changed": [], "foreman_note": null, "guard_result": null, "ci_result": null, "commit_hash": null, "perpetual": null, "created_at": "2026-09-24T03:55:24.297805+00:00", "updated_at": "2026-09-24T03:55:24.297805+00:00"}
 {"id": "AUG-057", "title": "--why-not silently keeps only its LAST value while --option repeats: a decision records one rejected reason and drops the rest", "priority": "P2", "complexity": 1, "status": "pending", "reasoning": "WHY (found live 2026-09-24, 5th dogfood run)\n\n`answer --option` is `action=\"append\"` (repeatable) but `--why-not` is a plain\nstring (auger.py, answer's add_argument block). Passing --why-not twice \u2014\nthe natural thing to do, since a decision rejects more than one alternative and\ndocs/VERBS.md lists `--why-not` beside the repeatable `--option` \u2014 silently keeps\nonly the last one. Exit 0.\n\nReproduced: an answer supplied two distinct rejected reasons (one per rejected\nalternative); `dump` rendered exactly one of them, the second.\n\nTwo harms: (a) the record loses reasoning the user believed they had stored \u2014\nwhich is the product's whole value proposition (\"remembers every choice and the\nreason the alternatives lost\"); (b) there is no shape in the schema to hold one\nreason PER OPTION, so recording it truthfully is not currently possible.\n\nDELIVERABLE\n- make the reason\u2192alternative relationship explicit rather than positional: either\n  --why-not repeats in lockstep with the rejected options (documented as such and\n  refused when the counts differ), or add a per-option reason form\n  (--why-not-for D-001-O2=\"...\") so each rejected option carries its own reason\n- whatever the shape, `dump` must render every recorded reason against the option\n  it belongs to\n- docs/VERBS.md must state the arity (this file is the contract; today it is\n  silent on whether --why-not repeats)\n\nACCEPTANCE CRITERIA\n- a decision rejecting two alternatives can record two reasons, both rendered by\n  `dump` against the right option\n- passing --why-not twice is either honoured or refused by name \u2014 never silently\n  truncated\n- docs/VERBS.md documents the arity of --why-not\n- regression test on the two-reason case", "capability_tags": ["+engine", "+docs"], "attempts": 0, "blocks": [], "depends_on": [], "detail": {}, "files_changed": [], "foreman_note": null, "guard_result": null, "ci_result": null, "commit_hash": null, "perpetual": null, "created_at": "2026-09-24T03:55:24.297805+00:00", "updated_at": "2026-09-24T03:55:24.297805+00:00"}
 {"id": "AUG-058", "title": "option.costs and option.breaks are declared columns no verb can ever set: dump prints costs=\u2014 breaks=\u2014 forever", "priority": "P2", "complexity": 1, "status": "pending", "reasoning": "WHY (found live 2026-09-24, 5th dogfood run)\n\nThe `option` table declares `costs` (varchar) and `breaks` (varchar) columns\n(auger.py COLS). `answer` inserts every option row with those fields as EMPTY\nSTRINGS and no verb anywhere in the CLI sets them:\n\n  grep for an add_argument covering costs/breaks across the whole\n  subparser registry -> zero hits.\n\nMeanwhile `dump` renders them for every option:\n\n  [ ] D-003-O1  <label>  costs=\u2014  breaks=\u2014\n\nSo the rendered configuration carries two fields that are structurally always\nempty. That is worse than omitting them: a reader of the dump sees \"costs=\u2014\" and\ncannot tell whether the option has no cost or auger simply cannot record one.\n\nThis is the option-level analogue of AUG-019 (registers break/assumption/unknown\nhave no CLI write path) and should probably be resolved with it \u2014 the same\nquestion, one table over.\n\nDELIVERABLE (decide, then make the code and the contract agree)\n- either give the fields a write path (`answer --option-cost` / `--option-breaks`,\n  or the per-option reason form AUG-057 proposes), and document them; OR\n- drop them from the declared table and from `dump`'s rendering, and say in\n  docs/VERBS.md why they were removed\n\nNeither answer is wrong; shipping a column no verb can fill and printing '\u2014' for\nit is.\n\nACCEPTANCE CRITERIA\n- `dump` never renders a field that no verb can populate\n- if the fields stay, a user can set them from the CLI and a test proves the value\n  round-trips into `dump`\n- docs/VERBS.md's `option` description matches whichever choice was made\n- the resolution is recorded on AUG-019 so the two register/option gaps close\n  consistently", "capability_tags": ["+decision", "+docs"], "attempts": 0, "blocks": [], "depends_on": [], "detail": {}, "files_changed": [], "foreman_note": null, "guard_result": null, "ci_result": null, "commit_hash": null, "perpetual": null, "created_at": "2026-09-24T03:55:24.297805+00:00", "updated_at": "2026-09-24T03:55:24.297805+00:00"}
